  user_input {
    user_response: "You are a senior product designer and front-end engineer. Build, in ONE go and without asking me any questions, a complete, polished, production-quality front-end web app called \"HAYAT\". Make every decision yourself, build every screen listed below, run the app, fix any errors you find, verify every screen and flow works, and only then report back with everything finished together. Do not give me partial work or manual steps.\n\n=====================================================\n1. WHAT HAYAT IS\n=====================================================\nHAYAT is a private, stigma-free \"front door\" for male reproductive health. It is built for the \"Future Health Digital Innovation Challenge\" (Abu Dhabi, by Future Health, Microsoft, Inception, MBZUAI), challenge: \"[Fertility] Male Reproductive Health\".\n\nThe problem: men usually enter the fertility pathway only after long difficulty conceiving or after the female partner has been assessed. This delays finding modifiable metabolic, infectious, anatomical or semen-related risk factors. A men\'s front door must reduce stigma, protect highly sensitive data, translate risk signals into appropriate clinical action, and respect religious, social and cultural sensitivities.\n\nHAYAT lets a man:\n1. Answer questions privately from his phone, with no name or login needed.\n2. Get an EXPLAINABLE risk screening (never a diagnosis).\n3. Be guided to the right next step: self-care, primary care, andrology/urology, fertility services, or urgent assessment.\n4. Control his data completely.\n\nThree non-negotiable principles, visible in the UI:\n- SCREENING, NOT DIAGNOSIS. Say this clearly on the results screen.\n- EXPLAINABLE. Every result shows exactly WHY.\n- PRIVACY BY DESIGN. Explicit consent, data minimisation, user-controlled sharing.\n\n=====================================================\n2. TECH STACK\n=====================================================\n- React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS\n- Framer Motion for animation, lucide-react for icons, Recharts for charts\n- react-router (HashRouter) for navigation\n- No real backend. Use a clean service layer with mock implementations (section 7) so a real backend can be plugged in later.\n- Do NOT call the Gemini API or any external API from the UI. Use template-based plain-language explanations.\n- Mobile-first, fully responsive, with polished desktop layout too.\n\n=====================================================\n3. DESIGN DIRECTION (must look premium and unique, NOT a generic template)\n=====================================================\nMood: calm, discreet, trustworthy, modern, quietly confident. Clinical-grade but warm. Not childish, not cold.\n\nPalette (define as CSS variables and Tailwind theme colors):\n- Background deep: #07131A (midnight teal-black)\n- Surface: #0D1F29, surface-2: #132B38, with subtle glassmorphism (backdrop-blur, 1px borders rgba(255,255,255,0.08))\n- Primary: #2DD4BF (teal-mint), primary glow gradient: #2DD4BF to #38BDF8\n- Warm accent: #E8D5B5 (sand), used for headings highlights and calm warmth\n- Text: #EAF4F4 main, #9FB4BA muted\n- Risk bands: Low #34D399, Moderate #FBBF24, Elevated #FB923C, Urgent #F87171\n- Add a light theme toggle (soft sand #F5EFE6 background, deep teal text) so judges can see both.\n\nTypography: \"Outfit\" for headings, \"Inter\" for body (Google Fonts). Large, confident hero type, generous spacing.\n\nVisual signatures:\n- A soft animated gradient-mesh / dotted wave background in the hero (a subtle flowing dot-wave pattern, echoing a data-signal motif), very low contrast so text stays readable.\n- Glass cards with soft glow on hover, rounded-2xl everywhere.\n- Animated circular risk gauge, animated bar charts, smooth step transitions, a gentle shimmer loading state.\n- A small custom logo: the word HAYAT with a minimal abstract mark (a circle with a soft wave line through it).\n- Micro-interactions on every button, toggle and card. Respect prefers-reduced-motion.\n- Accessibility: WCAG AA contrast, visible focus rings, full keyboard navigation, aria labels, large tap targets (min 44px).\n\n=====================================================\n4. GLOBAL FEATURES (present on every screen)\n=====================================================\nA) QUICK HIDE (discreet exit): a floating button top-right labelled with a neutral icon, plus the Escape key shortcut. One press instantly replaces the entire app with a harmless neutral \"Daily Wellness Tips\" screen (generic sleep/hydration/walking tips) so nobody nearby sees the content. A small \"Return\" link restores the app. This is a key stigma-reduction feature, so make it obvious and smooth.\n\nB) LANGUAGE SWITCHER: English, Hindi (\340\244\271\340\244\277\340\244\250\340\245\215\340\244\246\340\245\200), Arabic (\330\247\331\204\330\271\330\261\330\250\331\212\330\251). Implement a simple i18n dictionary file (src/i18n/en.ts, hi.ts, ar.ts). Arabic must switch the whole layout to RTL (dir=\"rtl\"). Translate ALL main UI strings: landing, consent, intake questions and options, results headings, triage labels, privacy dashboard, safety page. Keep wording gentle, respectful and non-judgemental in all three languages.\n\nC) CULTURAL SENSITIVITY: language must never assume marital status, religion or family choices. Use neutral phrasing such as \"if you are planning or trying for a family\", never \"your wife\". Use \"partner\" only where relevant and optional. Never use shaming words.\n\nD) SAFETY FOOTER on every screen: \"HAYAT is a screening and guidance tool. It does not diagnose, prescribe, or replace a clinician. A qualified healthcare professional makes every final decision.\" Plus a permanent small \"Prototype with simulated data\" badge.\n\nE) Top navigation: Home, Start Check, Care Navigator, Privacy Dashboard, Evidence, How It Works. Collapses to a clean bottom tab bar or hamburger on mobile.\n\n=====================================================\n5. SCREENS (build ALL of them)\n=====================================================\n\nSCREEN 1: LANDING (/)\n- Hero with headline like \"A private first step for men\'s reproductive health.\" Subline: \"No name. No login. No judgement. Just clear, explainable guidance in about 4 minutes.\"\n- Two buttons: \"Start my private check\" (primary) and \"How it works\".\n- Three trust pills: \"No account needed\", \"You control your data\", \"Explainable, not a black box\".\n- Section \"Why earlier matters\": 3 short cards (Many causes are modifiable / Early beats late / Assessment can start from home).\n- Section \"Built around trust\": 4 cards (Private by design, Culturally respectful, Clinically bounded, Human oversight).\n- A \"Try a demo case\" row with three demo persona buttons (Low risk / Moderate risk / Urgent red flag) that instantly pre-fill the whole assessment and jump to results. This is for the judges\' demo video, so make it prominent but tidy.\n\nSCREEN 2: CONSENT (/consent) shown before the assessment\n- Title \"Your data, your rules.\"\n- Two-column plain-language panel: \"What HAYAT does with your answers\" vs \"What HAYAT never does\".\n  Does: processes answers to produce your screening, keeps results only on your device unless you choose otherwise, lets you delete everything anytime.\n  Never: sells data, shares with employers/insurers/family, stores your name, makes a diagnosis.\n- Granular consent toggles, all OFF by default except the first one which is required:\n  1. (Required) \"Process my answers to generate my screening.\"\n  2. \"Save my results on this device so I can come back.\"\n  3. \"Share an anonymised summary to help improve fairness research.\"\n  4. \"Allow me to create a summary I can share with a clinician (I decide when).\"\n- A \"Data minimisation\" note: \"We only ask what is needed. Every question can be skipped.\"\n- Button \"I understand, continue\" disabled until toggle 1 is on. Record consent with a timestamp in app state.\n\nSCREEN 3: ASSESSMENT WIZARD (/check) \342\200\224 6 steps with a progress bar, back/next, autosave, step transitions\nEVERY question has: a \"Why we ask\" expandable tooltip and a \"Prefer not to say\" option. Never block progression on skipped questions.\nStep 1 \"About you\": age range (18-24, 25-29, 30-34, 35-39, 40-44, 45+), language is already chosen, \"Are you planning or trying for a family?\" (Not now / Planning / Trying).\nStep 2 \"Your journey\": if Trying, how long (under 3 months, 3-6, 6-12, over 12 months); \"Has a partner been assessed?\" (Yes / No / Not applicable / Prefer not to say); \"Any earlier fertility tests?\" (None / Semen analysis / Hormone tests / Other).\nStep 3 \"Daily life\": smoking/vaping (Never / Past / Occasional / Daily), alcohol (None / Light / Moderate / Heavy), sleep hours slider, exercise days per week slider, stress level 1-5, heat exposure (frequent hot baths/saunas, long laptop on lap, hot work environment: multi-select), supplements/anabolic steroid or testosterone use (None / Past / Current), recreational substance use (None / Occasional / Regular).\nStep 4 \"Body & metabolic\": height and weight (auto-compute BMI, show it softly), known conditions (multi-select: diabetes or high blood sugar, high blood pressure, thyroid issue, high cholesterol, none), waist concern (optional).\nStep 5 \"History & symptoms\": past mumps, past genital or urinary infection, any genital surgery or injury, undescended testicle as a child (all Yes/No/Not sure/Prefer not to say). Current symptoms (multi-select): a feeling of heaviness or swollen veins, a lump, dull ache, reduced sex drive, difficulty with erections, none. RED-FLAG symptoms (multi-select, shown with a calm amber style): sudden severe testicular pain, rapid swelling, fever with scrotal pain, visible blood in semen, a hard painless lump.\nStep 6 \"Test results (optional)\": \"Do you already have a semen analysis?\" If yes, inputs for volume (mL), concentration (million/mL), total motility (%), progressive motility (%), plus optional hormones (FSH, total testosterone) with units. Show a soft note: \"Skip this if you do not have results. HAYAT works without them.\"\nFinal button \"See my screening\".\n\nSCREEN 4: ANALYSING (transition, about 2.5 seconds)\nElegant animated screen with rotating reassurance lines (\"Weighing your lifestyle factors\", \"Checking for red flags first\", \"Preparing explanations in plain language\"). Red-flag check message appears first to signal safety-first design.\n\nSCREEN 5: RESULTS (/results) \342\200\224 the hero screen, make it spectacular\nTop: a clear amber/blue banner \"This is a screening, not a diagnosis.\"\nSection A, Risk Overview: animated circular gauge with the risk band (Low / Moderate / Elevated / Urgent) coloured per palette, plus a 1-sentence plain-language summary. Show the score as a band first; the numeric score is secondary.\nSection B, \"Why this result?\" (EXPLAINABILITY PANEL): a horizontal contribution bar chart of the top factors, each with direction (raises / lowers / neutral), a short plain-language explanation, a tag \"You can change this\" or \"Not changeable\", and the value the user gave. Toggle between \"Simple\" and \"Detailed\" explanation views. Also list \"Factors working in your favour\". Include a \"What we did NOT use\" line (name, religion, location).\nSection C, Screening vs Diagnosis card: three short points on what this result can and cannot tell him.\nSection D, Red flags: if any red flag is selected, show a prominent, calm but firm urgent card at the very top of the page with \"Please seek medical assessment today\" and why. Never hide this.\nSection E, Recommended next step (TRIAGE): a 5-level ladder (Self-care \342\206\222 Primary care \342\206\222 Andrology/Urology \342\206\222 Fertility service \342\206\222 Urgent assessment) with the recommended level highlighted and a one-line reason. Beneath it, \"Clinical boundaries and human oversight\": states which cases always require a human clinician, and that HAYAT never replaces one.\nSection F, Action plan: \"Things you can start this week\" (3-5 modifiable-factor actions tailored to the answers) and \"Questions to ask a doctor\" list.\nSection G, Buttons: \"Open Care Navigator\", \"Create clinician summary (you decide who sees it)\" which opens a clean printable summary modal with a Print/Save-as-PDF button, only enabled if consent toggle 4 was on (otherwise prompt to enable it), and \"Delete my data now\".\n\nSCREEN 6: CARE NAVIGATOR (/care)\n- Step-by-step pathway cards matching the triage level, with \"What to expect\", \"Typical visit\", \"How to prepare\".\n- Low-friction options: \"Private teleconsultation\" (mock booking widget with date/time slots and a discreet-appointment-label option), \"At-home steps where clinically appropriate\" (clear note that clinician confirms suitability), \"Find a clinic\" (mock list of 4 clinics with distance, services, \"male-friendly / private entrance / female or male clinician preference\" filters).\n- Referral tracker: a simple status timeline (Screened \342\206\222 Booked \342\206\222 Assessed \342\206\222 Follow-up) with mark-as-done buttons, saved locally. This supports the \"referral completion\" metric.\n\nSCREEN 7: PRIVACY DASHBOARD (/privacy)\n- \"Everything HAYAT holds about you\" data inventory table (field, value, stored where, purpose) with per-item delete.\n- Consent toggles (same four) that can be changed any time, with an immediate effect.\n- Buttons: \"Export my data (JSON)\", \"Delete everything now\" (confirmation modal, then wipes all local state and shows a success state).\n- Access log: a simple list of events (\"Assessment created\", \"Summary generated\", \"Consent changed\") with timestamps.\n- A plain-language \"How your data is protected\" accordion (data minimisation, no identifiers, encryption planned in transit and at rest in the full version, access controls, retention limits).\n\nSCREEN 8: EVIDENCE (/evidence) \342\200\224 demonstrates value\nClearly labelled \"SIMULATED EVALUATION: 500 synthetic cases, not real patients\".\n- KPI cards (animated count-up): Assessment completion rate (e.g. 91%), Median time to appropriate evaluation (before 7.5 months vs with HAYAT 2.5 months), Referral completion rate (e.g. 68%), User trust score (e.g. 4.5/5), Red-flag recall (e.g. 100% of simulated red-flag cases routed to urgent).\n- Charts (Recharts): completion funnel by step, time-to-evaluation before vs after, triage distribution of the 500 cases, and a FAIRNESS section: grouped bar charts of completion rate, correct triage rate and trust score across age groups (18-29, 30-39, 40+) and across language groups (English, Hindi, Arabic), with a \"max gap\" indicator and a green tick if the gap is under 5 percentage points.\n- A \"Method\" accordion explaining the synthetic data generation and metrics honestly.\n- Generate this data from a seeded deterministic function in src/data/syntheticCohort.ts so it is reproducible, and expose it via the service layer.\n\nSCREEN 9: HOW IT WORKS / SAFETY (/how-it-works)\n- Animated 4-step explainer.\n- \"How the risk screening works\": transparent list of the rule categories and a plain statement that it is a rule-based, explainable model, not a black box.\n- \"Safety and limits\": what HAYAT can and cannot do, red-flag list, when to call a doctor immediately, and the human-oversight policy.\n- \"Fairness and cultural respect\" section.\n- FAQ accordion (8 questions, including \"Is this a diagnosis?\", \"Who can see my data?\", \"Why do you ask about lifestyle?\").\n\nSCREEN 10: QUICK-HIDE neutral screen (described in 4A).\n\n=====================================================\n6. RISK ENGINE (src/engine/riskEngine.ts) \342\200\224 transparent and explainable\n=====================================================\nWrite a pure, well-typed, well-commented function:\nassess(answers) => { band, score (0-100), factors[], redFlags[], triage, favourableFactors[], missingInfoNote }\n\nEach factor object: { id, label, direction: \'raises\'|\'lowers\'|\'neutral\', weight (number), modifiable (boolean), userValue (string), explanation (plain language, gentle) }.\n\nRED FLAGS (checked FIRST and override everything): sudden severe testicular pain, rapid swelling, fever with scrotal pain, visible blood in semen, hard painless lump. Any red flag sets band = \"Urgent\" and triage = \"Urgent assessment\". Pain with swelling is time-critical, so wording must say seek care today.\n\nRule-based weights (sum, then cap 0-100) for these factors, using simple general-knowledge reasoning:\n- Trying 12+ months (strong), 6-12 months (moderate)\n- Age 40+ (mild), 45+ (mild-moderate)\n- Daily smoking (moderate), heavy alcohol (moderate), moderate alcohol (mild)\n- BMI 30+ (moderate), BMI 25-29.9 (mild), BMI under 18.5 (mild)\n- Diabetes/high blood sugar (moderate), thyroid issue (mild)\n- Frequent heat exposure (mild)\n- Current/past anabolic steroid or testosterone use (strong when current)\n- Regular recreational substance use (mild-moderate)\n- Poor sleep under 6h (mild), high stress 4-5 (mild)\n- Past mumps (mild), past genital/urinary infection (moderate), surgery/injury (mild), undescended testicle history (moderate)\n- Symptoms of heaviness/swollen veins (moderate, may suggest a varicocele), dull ache (mild)\n- Semen analysis, using WHO 2021 lower reference limits: volume under 1.4 mL, concentration under 16 million/mL, total motility under 42%, progressive motility under 30% each add significant weight if entered. Words used must say \"below the reference range\", never \"infertile\".\n- Protective/lowering factors: regular exercise 3+ days, non-smoker, healthy BMI 18.5-24.9, good sleep, low stress.\nBands: 0-24 Low, 25-49 Moderate, 50-74 Elevated, 75+ Elevated-high still \"Elevated\" (Urgent is ONLY from red flags).\nTriage mapping: Low \342\206\222 Self-care; Moderate \342\206\222 Primary care; Elevated \342\206\222 Andrology/Urology; Elevated + trying 12+ months or abnormal semen values \342\206\222 Fertility service; any red flag \342\206\222 Urgent assessment.\nAlways include a missingInfoNote when optional sections were skipped (\"This screening is based on what you shared. More information can make it more accurate.\").\nNEVER output words like \"you have\", \"diagnosed with\", \"infertile\", \"impotent\". Use screening language only.\n\nAlso provide 3 demo personas (src/data/personas.ts): Low risk (29, healthy, trying 2 months), Moderate risk (36, BMI 28, smoker, trying 8 months), Urgent (31, sudden severe pain and swelling flagged).\n\n=====================================================\n7. SERVICE LAYER (so my backend can be plugged in later)\n=====================================================\nCreate src/services/api.ts exporting async functions with realistic delays and clear \"TODO: replace with real backend endpoint\" comments, each with typed request/response interfaces in src/types:\n- assess(answers): POST /api/assess\n- explain(result, mode, language): POST /api/explain (returns plain-language explanation text; mock uses templates)\n- saveConsent(consent): POST /api/consent\n- getConsentLog(): GET /api/consent-log\n- deleteAllData(): DELETE /api/data\n- exportData(): GET /api/export\n- getEvidence(): GET /api/evidence (returns the synthetic cohort metrics)\n- getClinics(filters): GET /api/clinics\n- bookTeleconsult(slot): POST /api/teleconsult\nRead the base URL from import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE with a fallback to mock mode, with a single flag VITE_USE_MOCK=true. State lives in a small store (Zustand or React Context) persisted to localStorage ONLY if consent toggle 2 is on; otherwise memory only.\n\n=====================================================\n8. CODE QUALITY AND STRUCTURE\n=====================================================\nFolder structure: src/{components, pages, engine, services, data, i18n, store, hooks, types, styles}. Reusable components (GlassCard, Gauge, StepWizard, ConsentToggle, FactorBar, TriageLadder, QuickHide, LangSwitcher, KpiCard, SafetyFooter). Strong TypeScript types, no any. Include a README.md describing the app, structure, how to run, and the mock-to-backend swap. Include a .env.example.\n\n=====================================================\n9. FINISH CHECKLIST (do ALL of this yourself before replying)\n=====================================================\n1. Run the app and make sure there are zero console errors and zero TypeScript errors.\n2. Test every screen and flow: landing \342\206\222 consent \342\206\222 all 6 wizard steps \342\206\222 analysing \342\206\222 results; all three demo personas; red-flag override; Quick Hide and Escape key; language switch including Arabic RTL; light/dark theme; privacy dashboard delete and export; clinician summary modal; evidence charts.\n3. Check mobile (375px) and desktop (1440px) layouts and fix any overflow or overlap.\n4. Fix every issue you find yourself.\n5. Then reply with ONE final message: a short summary of what was built, the file structure, and anything I should know. Do not ask me questions."
    items {
      text: "You are a senior product designer and front-end engineer. Build, in ONE go and without asking me any questions, a complete, polished, production-quality front-end web app called \"HAYAT\". Make every decision yourself, build every screen listed below, run the app, fix any errors you find, verify every screen and flow works, and only then report back with everything finished together. Do not give me partial work or manual steps.\n\n=====================================================\n1. WHAT HAYAT IS\n=====================================================\nHAYAT is a private, stigma-free \"front door\" for male reproductive health. It is built for the \"Future Health Digital Innovation Challenge\" (Abu Dhabi, by Future Health, Microsoft, Inception, MBZUAI), challenge: \"[Fertility] Male Reproductive Health\".\n\nThe problem: men usually enter the fertility pathway only after long difficulty conceiving or after the female partner has been assessed. This delays finding modifiable metabolic, infectious, anatomical or semen-related risk factors. A men\'s front door must reduce stigma, protect highly sensitive data, translate risk signals into appropriate clinical action, and respect religious, social and cultural sensitivities.\n\nHAYAT lets a man:\n1. Answer questions privately from his phone, with no name or login needed.\n2. Get an EXPLAINABLE risk screening (never a diagnosis).\n3. Be guided to the right next step: self-care, primary care, andrology/urology, fertility services, or urgent assessment.\n4. Control his data completely.\n\nThree non-negotiable principles, visible in the UI:\n- SCREENING, NOT DIAGNOSIS. Say this clearly on the results screen.\n- EXPLAINABLE. Every result shows exactly WHY.\n- PRIVACY BY DESIGN. Explicit consent, data minimisation, user-controlled sharing.\n\n=====================================================\n2. TECH STACK\n=====================================================\n- React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS\n- Framer Motion for animation, lucide-react for icons, Recharts for charts\n- react-router (HashRouter) for navigation\n- No real backend. Use a clean service layer with mock implementations (section 7) so a real backend can be plugged in later.\n- Do NOT call the Gemini API or any external API from the UI. Use template-based plain-language explanations.\n- Mobile-first, fully responsive, with polished desktop layout too.\n\n=====================================================\n3. DESIGN DIRECTION (must look premium and unique, NOT a generic template)\n=====================================================\nMood: calm, discreet, trustworthy, modern, quietly confident. Clinical-grade but warm. Not childish, not cold.\n\nPalette (define as CSS variables and Tailwind theme colors):\n- Background deep: #07131A (midnight teal-black)\n- Surface: #0D1F29, surface-2: #132B38, with subtle glassmorphism (backdrop-blur, 1px borders rgba(255,255,255,0.08))\n- Primary: #2DD4BF (teal-mint), primary glow gradient: #2DD4BF to #38BDF8\n- Warm accent: #E8D5B5 (sand), used for headings highlights and calm warmth\n- Text: #EAF4F4 main, #9FB4BA muted\n- Risk bands: Low #34D399, Moderate #FBBF24, Elevated #FB923C, Urgent #F87171\n- Add a light theme toggle (soft sand #F5EFE6 background, deep teal text) so judges can see both.\n\nTypography: \"Outfit\" for headings, \"Inter\" for body (Google Fonts). Large, confident hero type, generous spacing.\n\nVisual signatures:\n- A soft animated gradient-mesh / dotted wave background in the hero (a subtle flowing dot-wave pattern, echoing a data-signal motif), very low contrast so text stays readable.\n- Glass cards with soft glow on hover, rounded-2xl everywhere.\n- Animated circular risk gauge, animated bar charts, smooth step transitions, a gentle shimmer loading state.\n- A small custom logo: the word HAYAT with a minimal abstract mark (a circle with a soft wave line through it).\n- Micro-interactions on every button, toggle and card. Respect prefers-reduced-motion.\n- Accessibility: WCAG AA contrast, visible focus rings, full keyboard navigation, aria labels, large tap targets (min 44px).\n\n=====================================================\n4. GLOBAL FEATURES (present on every screen)\n=====================================================\nA) QUICK HIDE (discreet exit): a floating button top-right labelled with a neutral icon, plus the Escape key shortcut. One press instantly replaces the entire app with a harmless neutral \"Daily Wellness Tips\" screen (generic sleep/hydration/walking tips) so nobody nearby sees the content. A small \"Return\" link restores the app. This is a key stigma-reduction feature, so make it obvious and smooth.\n\nB) LANGUAGE SWITCHER: English, Hindi (\340\244\271\340\244\277\340\244\250\340\245\215\340\244\246\340\245\200), Arabic (\330\247\331\204\330\271\330\261\330\250\331\212\330\251). Implement a simple i18n dictionary file (src/i18n/en.ts, hi.ts, ar.ts). Arabic must switch the whole layout to RTL (dir=\"rtl\"). Translate ALL main UI strings: landing, consent, intake questions and options, results headings, triage labels, privacy dashboard, safety page. Keep wording gentle, respectful and non-judgemental in all three languages.\n\nC) CULTURAL SENSITIVITY: language must never assume marital status, religion or family choices. Use neutral phrasing such as \"if you are planning or trying for a family\", never \"your wife\". Use \"partner\" only where relevant and optional. Never use shaming words.\n\nD) SAFETY FOOTER on every screen: \"HAYAT is a screening and guidance tool. It does not diagnose, prescribe, or replace a clinician. A qualified healthcare professional makes every final decision.\" Plus a permanent small \"Prototype with simulated data\" badge.\n\nE) Top navigation: Home, Start Check, Care Navigator, Privacy Dashboard, Evidence, How It Works. Collapses to a clean bottom tab bar or hamburger on mobile.\n\n=====================================================\n5. SCREENS (build ALL of them)\n=====================================================\n\nSCREEN 1: LANDING (/)\n- Hero with headline like \"A private first step for men\'s reproductive health.\" Subline: \"No name. No login. No judgement. Just clear, explainable guidance in about 4 minutes.\"\n- Two buttons: \"Start my private check\" (primary) and \"How it works\".\n- Three trust pills: \"No account needed\", \"You control your data\", \"Explainable, not a black box\".\n- Section \"Why earlier matters\": 3 short cards (Many causes are modifiable / Early beats late / Assessment can start from home).\n- Section \"Built around trust\": 4 cards (Private by design, Culturally respectful, Clinically bounded, Human oversight).\n- A \"Try a demo case\" row with three demo persona buttons (Low risk / Moderate risk / Urgent red flag) that instantly pre-fill the whole assessment and jump to results. This is for the judges\' demo video, so make it prominent but tidy.\n\nSCREEN 2: CONSENT (/consent) shown before the assessment\n- Title \"Your data, your rules.\"\n- Two-column plain-language panel: \"What HAYAT does with your answers\" vs \"What HAYAT never does\".\n  Does: processes answers to produce your screening, keeps results only on your device unless you choose otherwise, lets you delete everything anytime.\n  Never: sells data, shares with employers/insurers/family, stores your name, makes a diagnosis.\n- Granular consent toggles, all OFF by default except the first one which is required:\n  1. (Required) \"Process my answers to generate my screening.\"\n  2. \"Save my results on this device so I can come back.\"\n  3. \"Share an anonymised summary to help improve fairness research.\"\n  4. \"Allow me to create a summary I can share with a clinician (I decide when).\"\n- A \"Data minimisation\" note: \"We only ask what is needed. Every question can be skipped.\"\n- Button \"I understand, continue\" disabled until toggle 1 is on. Record consent with a timestamp in app state.\n\nSCREEN 3: ASSESSMENT WIZARD (/check) \342\200\224 6 steps with a progress bar, back/next, autosave, step transitions\nEVERY question has: a \"Why we ask\" expandable tooltip and a \"Prefer not to say\" option. Never block progression on skipped questions.\nStep 1 \"About you\": age range (18-24, 25-29, 30-34, 35-39, 40-44, 45+), language is already chosen, \"Are you planning or trying for a family?\" (Not now / Planning / Trying).\nStep 2 \"Your journey\": if Trying, how long (under 3 months, 3-6, 6-12, over 12 months); \"Has a partner been assessed?\" (Yes / No / Not applicable / Prefer not to say); \"Any earlier fertility tests?\" (None / Semen analysis / Hormone tests / Other).\nStep 3 \"Daily life\": smoking/vaping (Never / Past / Occasional / Daily), alcohol (None / Light / Moderate / Heavy), sleep hours slider, exercise days per week slider, stress level 1-5, heat exposure (frequent hot baths/saunas, long laptop on lap, hot work environment: multi-select), supplements/anabolic steroid or testosterone use (None / Past / Current), recreational substance use (None / Occasional / Regular).\nStep 4 \"Body & metabolic\": height and weight (auto-compute BMI, show it softly), known conditions (multi-select: diabetes or high blood sugar, high blood pressure, thyroid issue, high cholesterol, none), waist concern (optional).\nStep 5 \"History & symptoms\": past mumps, past genital or urinary infection, any genital surgery or injury, undescended testicle as a child (all Yes/No/Not sure/Prefer not to say). Current symptoms (multi-select): a feeling of heaviness or swollen veins, a lump, dull ache, reduced sex drive, difficulty with erections, none. RED-FLAG symptoms (multi-select, shown with a calm amber style): sudden severe testicular pain, rapid swelling, fever with scrotal pain, visible blood in semen, a hard painless lump.\nStep 6 \"Test results (optional)\": \"Do you already have a semen analysis?\" If yes, inputs for volume (mL), concentration (million/mL), total motility (%), progressive motility (%), plus optional hormones (FSH, total testosterone) with units. Show a soft note: \"Skip this if you do not have results. HAYAT works without them.\"\nFinal button \"See my screening\".\n\nSCREEN 4: ANALYSING (transition, about 2.5 seconds)\nElegant animated screen with rotating reassurance lines (\"Weighing your lifestyle factors\", \"Checking for red flags first\", \"Preparing explanations in plain language\"). Red-flag check message appears first to signal safety-first design.\n\nSCREEN 5: RESULTS (/results) \342\200\224 the hero screen, make it spectacular\nTop: a clear amber/blue banner \"This is a screening, not a diagnosis.\"\nSection A, Risk Overview: animated circular gauge with the risk band (Low / Moderate / Elevated / Urgent) coloured per palette, plus a 1-sentence plain-language summary. Show the score as a band first; the numeric score is secondary.\nSection B, \"Why this result?\" (EXPLAINABILITY PANEL): a horizontal contribution bar chart of the top factors, each with direction (raises / lowers / neutral), a short plain-language explanation, a tag \"You can change this\" or \"Not changeable\", and the value the user gave. Toggle between \"Simple\" and \"Detailed\" explanation views. Also list \"Factors working in your favour\". Include a \"What we did NOT use\" line (name, religion, location).\nSection C, Screening vs Diagnosis card: three short points on what this result can and cannot tell him.\nSection D, Red flags: if any red flag is selected, show a prominent, calm but firm urgent card at the very top of the page with \"Please seek medical assessment today\" and why. Never hide this.\nSection E, Recommended next step (TRIAGE): a 5-level ladder (Self-care \342\206\222 Primary care \342\206\222 Andrology/Urology \342\206\222 Fertility service \342\206\222 Urgent assessment) with the recommended level highlighted and a one-line reason. Beneath it, \"Clinical boundaries and human oversight\": states which cases always require a human clinician, and that HAYAT never replaces one.\nSection F, Action plan: \"Things you can start this week\" (3-5 modifiable-factor actions tailored to the answers) and \"Questions to ask a doctor\" list.\nSection G, Buttons: \"Open Care Navigator\", \"Create clinician summary (you decide who sees it)\" which opens a clean printable summary modal with a Print/Save-as-PDF button, only enabled if consent toggle 4 was on (otherwise prompt to enable it), and \"Delete my data now\".\n\nSCREEN 6: CARE NAVIGATOR (/care)\n- Step-by-step pathway cards matching the triage level, with \"What to expect\", \"Typical visit\", \"How to prepare\".\n- Low-friction options: \"Private teleconsultation\" (mock booking widget with date/time slots and a discreet-appointment-label option), \"At-home steps where clinically appropriate\" (clear note that clinician confirms suitability), \"Find a clinic\" (mock list of 4 clinics with distance, services, \"male-friendly / private entrance / female or male clinician preference\" filters).\n- Referral tracker: a simple status timeline (Screened \342\206\222 Booked \342\206\222 Assessed \342\206\222 Follow-up) with mark-as-done buttons, saved locally. This supports the \"referral completion\" metric.\n\nSCREEN 7: PRIVACY DASHBOARD (/privacy)\n- \"Everything HAYAT holds about you\" data inventory table (field, value, stored where, purpose) with per-item delete.\n- Consent toggles (same four) that can be changed any time, with an immediate effect.\n- Buttons: \"Export my data (JSON)\", \"Delete everything now\" (confirmation modal, then wipes all local state and shows a success state).\n- Access log: a simple list of events (\"Assessment created\", \"Summary generated\", \"Consent changed\") with timestamps.\n- A plain-language \"How your data is protected\" accordion (data minimisation, no identifiers, encryption planned in transit and at rest in the full version, access controls, retention limits).\n\nSCREEN 8: EVIDENCE (/evidence) \342\200\224 demonstrates value\nClearly labelled \"SIMULATED EVALUATION: 500 synthetic cases, not real patients\".\n- KPI cards (animated count-up): Assessment completion rate (e.g. 91%), Median time to appropriate evaluation (before 7.5 months vs with HAYAT 2.5 months), Referral completion rate (e.g. 68%), User trust score (e.g. 4.5/5), Red-flag recall (e.g. 100% of simulated red-flag cases routed to urgent).\n- Charts (Recharts): completion funnel by step, time-to-evaluation before vs after, triage distribution of the 500 cases, and a FAIRNESS section: grouped bar charts of completion rate, correct triage rate and trust score across age groups (18-29, 30-39, 40+) and across language groups (English, Hindi, Arabic), with a \"max gap\" indicator and a green tick if the gap is under 5 percentage points.\n- A \"Method\" accordion explaining the synthetic data generation and metrics honestly.\n- Generate this data from a seeded deterministic function in src/data/syntheticCohort.ts so it is reproducible, and expose it via the service layer.\n\nSCREEN 9: HOW IT WORKS / SAFETY (/how-it-works)\n- Animated 4-step explainer.\n- \"How the risk screening works\": transparent list of the rule categories and a plain statement that it is a rule-based, explainable model, not a black box.\n- \"Safety and limits\": what HAYAT can and cannot do, red-flag list, when to call a doctor immediately, and the human-oversight policy.\n- \"Fairness and cultural respect\" section.\n- FAQ accordion (8 questions, including \"Is this a diagnosis?\", \"Who can see my data?\", \"Why do you ask about lifestyle?\").\n\nSCREEN 10: QUICK-HIDE neutral screen (described in 4A).\n\n=====================================================\n6. RISK ENGINE (src/engine/riskEngine.ts) \342\200\224 transparent and explainable\n=====================================================\nWrite a pure, well-typed, well-commented function:\nassess(answers) => { band, score (0-100), factors[], redFlags[], triage, favourableFactors[], missingInfoNote }\n\nEach factor object: { id, label, direction: \'raises\'|\'lowers\'|\'neutral\', weight (number), modifiable (boolean), userValue (string), explanation (plain language, gentle) }.\n\nRED FLAGS (checked FIRST and override everything): sudden severe testicular pain, rapid swelling, fever with scrotal pain, visible blood in semen, hard painless lump. Any red flag sets band = \"Urgent\" and triage = \"Urgent assessment\". Pain with swelling is time-critical, so wording must say seek care today.\n\nRule-based weights (sum, then cap 0-100) for these factors, using simple general-knowledge reasoning:\n- Trying 12+ months (strong), 6-12 months (moderate)\n- Age 40+ (mild), 45+ (mild-moderate)\n- Daily smoking (moderate), heavy alcohol (moderate), moderate alcohol (mild)\n- BMI 30+ (moderate), BMI 25-29.9 (mild), BMI under 18.5 (mild)\n- Diabetes/high blood sugar (moderate), thyroid issue (mild)\n- Frequent heat exposure (mild)\n- Current/past anabolic steroid or testosterone use (strong when current)\n- Regular recreational substance use (mild-moderate)\n- Poor sleep under 6h (mild), high stress 4-5 (mild)\n- Past mumps (mild), past genital/urinary infection (moderate), surgery/injury (mild), undescended testicle history (moderate)\n- Symptoms of heaviness/swollen veins (moderate, may suggest a varicocele), dull ache (mild)\n- Semen analysis, using WHO 2021 lower reference limits: volume under 1.4 mL, concentration under 16 million/mL, total motility under 42%, progressive motility under 30% each add significant weight if entered. Words used must say \"below the reference range\", never \"infertile\".\n- Protective/lowering factors: regular exercise 3+ days, non-smoker, healthy BMI 18.5-24.9, good sleep, low stress.\nBands: 0-24 Low, 25-49 Moderate, 50-74 Elevated, 75+ Elevated-high still \"Elevated\" (Urgent is ONLY from red flags).\nTriage mapping: Low \342\206\222 Self-care; Moderate \342\206\222 Primary care; Elevated \342\206\222 Andrology/Urology; Elevated + trying 12+ months or abnormal semen values \342\206\222 Fertility service; any red flag \342\206\222 Urgent assessment.\nAlways include a missingInfoNote when optional sections were skipped (\"This screening is based on what you shared. More information can make it more accurate.\").\nNEVER output words like \"you have\", \"diagnosed with\", \"infertile\", \"impotent\". Use screening language only.\n\nAlso provide 3 demo personas (src/data/personas.ts): Low risk (29, healthy, trying 2 months), Moderate risk (36, BMI 28, smoker, trying 8 months), Urgent (31, sudden severe pain and swelling flagged).\n\n=====================================================\n7. SERVICE LAYER (so my backend can be plugged in later)\n=====================================================\nCreate src/services/api.ts exporting async functions with realistic delays and clear \"TODO: replace with real backend endpoint\" comments, each with typed request/response interfaces in src/types:\n- assess(answers): POST /api/assess\n- explain(result, mode, language): POST /api/explain (returns plain-language explanation text; mock uses templates)\n- saveConsent(consent): POST /api/consent\n- getConsentLog(): GET /api/consent-log\n- deleteAllData(): DELETE /api/data\n- exportData(): GET /api/export\n- getEvidence(): GET /api/evidence (returns the synthetic cohort metrics)\n- getClinics(filters): GET /api/clinics\n- bookTeleconsult(slot): POST /api/teleconsult\nRead the base URL from import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE with a fallback to mock mode, with a single flag VITE_USE_MOCK=true. State lives in a small store (Zustand or React Context) persisted to localStorage ONLY if consent toggle 2 is on; otherwise memory only.\n\n=====================================================\n8. CODE QUALITY AND STRUCTURE\n=====================================================\nFolder structure: src/{components, pages, engine, services, data, i18n, store, hooks, types, styles}. Reusable components (GlassCard, Gauge, StepWizard, ConsentToggle, FactorBar, TriageLadder, QuickHide, LangSwitcher, KpiCard, SafetyFooter). Strong TypeScript types, no any. Include a README.md describing the app, structure, how to run, and the mock-to-backend swap. Include a .env.example.\n\n=====================================================\n9. FINISH CHECKLIST (do ALL of this yourself before replying)\n=====================================================\n1. Run the app and make sure there are zero console errors and zero TypeScript errors.\n2. Test every screen and flow: landing \342\206\222 consent \342\206\222 all 6 wizard steps \342\206\222 analysing \342\206\222 results; all three demo personas; red-flag override; Quick Hide and Escape key; language switch including Arabic RTL; light/dark theme; privacy dashboard delete and export; clinician summary modal; evidence charts.\n3. Check mobile (375px) and desktop (1440px) layouts and fix any overflow or overlap.\n4. Fix every issue you find yourself.\n5. Then reply with ONE final message: a short summary of what was built, the file structure, and anything I should know. Do not ask me questions."
    }
  }
}
