    total_subagent_count: 0
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Here is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.3.3\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^6.1.1\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^8.3.0\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\n@types/react: ^19.3.0\n@types/react-dom: ^19.3.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.3.3\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ^7.0.2\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(<App />);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\"],\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  },\n  \"exclude\": [\"dist\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\342\200\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "You are a senior product designer and front-end engineer. Build, in ONE go and without asking me any questions, a complete, polished, production-quality front-end web app called \"HAYAT\". Make every decision yourself, build every screen listed below, run the app, fix any errors you find, verify every screen and flow works, and only then report back with everything finished together. Do not give me partial work or manual steps.\n\n=====================================================\n1. WHAT HAYAT IS\n=====================================================\nHAYAT is a private, stigma-free \"front door\" for male reproductive health. It is built for the \"Future Health Digital Innovation Challenge\" (Abu Dhabi, by Future Health, Microsoft, Inception, MBZUAI), challenge: \"[Fertility] Male Reproductive Health\".\n\nThe problem: men usually enter the fertility pathway only after long difficulty conceiving or after the female partner has been assessed. This delays finding modifiable metabolic, infectious, anatomical or semen-related risk factors. A men\'s front door must reduce stigma, protect highly sensitive data, translate risk signals into appropriate clinical action, and respect religious, social and cultural sensitivities.\n\nHAYAT lets a man:\n1. Answer questions privately from his phone, with no name or login needed.\n2. Get an EXPLAINABLE risk screening (never a diagnosis).\n3. Be guided to the right next step: self-care, primary care, andrology/urology, fertility services, or urgent assessment.\n4. Control his data completely.\n\nThree non-negotiable principles, visible in the UI:\n- SCREENING, NOT DIAGNOSIS. Say this clearly on the results screen.\n- EXPLAINABLE. Every result shows exactly WHY.\n- PRIVACY BY DESIGN. Explicit consent, data minimisation, user-controlled sharing.\n\n=====================================================\n2. TECH STACK\n=====================================================\n- React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS\n- Framer Motion for animation, lucide-react for icons, Recharts for charts\n- react-router (HashRouter) for navigation\n- No real backend. Use a clean service layer with mock implementations (section 7) so a real backend can be plugged in later.\n- Do NOT call the Gemini API or any external API from the UI. Use template-based plain-language explanations.\n- Mobile-first, fully responsive, with polished desktop layout too.\n\n=====================================================\n3. DESIGN DIRECTION (must look premium and unique, NOT a generic template)\n=====================================================\nMood: calm, discreet, trustworthy, modern, quietly confident. Clinical-grade but warm. Not childish, not cold.\n\nPalette (define as CSS variables and Tailwind theme colors):\n- Background deep: #07131A (midnight teal-black)\n- Surface: #0D1F29, surface-2: #132B38, with subtle glassmorphism (backdrop-blur, 1px borders rgba(255,255,255,0.08))\n- Primary: #2DD4BF (teal-mint), primary glow gradient: #2DD4BF to #38BDF8\n- Warm accent: #E8D5B5 (sand), used for headings highlights and calm warmth\n- Text: #EAF4F4 main, #9FB4BA muted\n- Risk bands: Low #34D399, Moderate #FBBF24, Elevated #FB923C, Urgent #F87171\n- Add a light theme toggle (soft sand #F5EFE6 background, deep teal text) so judges can see both.\n\nTypography: \"Outfit\" for headings, \"Inter\" for body (Google Fonts). Large, confident hero type, generous spacing.\n\nVisual signatures:\n- A soft animated gradient-mesh / dotted wave background in the hero (a subtle flowing dot-wave pattern, echoing a data-signal motif), very low contrast so text stays readable.\n- Glass cards with soft glow on hover, rounded-2xl everywhere.\n- Animated circular risk gauge, animated bar charts, smooth step transitions, a gentle shimmer loading state.\n- A small custom logo: the word HAYAT with a minimal abstract mark (a circle with a soft wave line through it).\n- Micro-interactions on every button, toggle and card. Respect prefers-reduced-motion.\n- Accessibility: WCAG AA contrast, visible focus rings, full keyboard navigation, aria labels, large tap targets (min 44px).\n\n=====================================================\n4. GLOBAL FEATURES (present on every screen)\n=====================================================\nA) QUICK HIDE (discreet exit): a floating button top-right labelled with a neutral icon, plus the Escape key shortcut. One press instantly replaces the entire app with a harmless neutral \"Daily Wellness Tips\" screen (generic sleep/hydration/walking tips) so nobody nearby sees the content. A small \"Return\" link restores the app. This is a key stigma-reduction feature, so make it obvious and smooth.\n\nB) LANGUAGE SWITCHER: English, Hindi (\340\244\271\340\244\277\340\244\250\340\245\215\340\244\246\340\245\200), Arabic (\330\247\331\204\330\271\330\261\330\250\331\212\330\251). Implement a simple i18n dictionary file (src/i18n/en.ts, hi.ts, ar.ts). Arabic must switch the whole layout to RTL (dir=\"rtl\"). Translate ALL main UI strings: landing, consent, intake questions and options, results headings, triage labels, privacy dashboard, safety page. Keep wording gentle, respectful and non-judgemental in all three languages.\n\nC) CULTURAL SENSITIVITY: language must never assume marital status, religion or family choices. Use neutral phrasing such as \"if you are planning or trying for a family\", never \"your wife\". Use \"partner\" only where relevant and optional. Never use shaming words.\n\nD) SAFETY FOOTER on every screen: \"HAYAT is a screening and guidance tool. It does not diagnose, prescribe, or replace a clinician. A qualified healthcare professional makes every final decision.\" Plus a permanent small \"Prototype with simulated data\" badge.\n\nE) Top navigation: Home, Start Check, Care Navigator, Privacy Dashboard, Evidence, How It Works. Collapses to a clean bottom tab bar or hamburger on mobile.\n\n=====================================================\n5. SCREENS (build ALL of them)\n=====================================================\n\nSCREEN 1: LANDING (/)\n- Hero with headline like \"A private first step for men\'s reproductive health.\" Subline: \"No name. No login. No judgement. Just clear, explainable guidance in about 4 minutes.\"\n- Two buttons: \"Start my private check\" (primary) and \"How it works\".\n- Three trust pills: \"No account needed\", \"You control your data\", \"Explainable, not a black box\".\n- Section \"Why earlier matters\": 3 short cards (Many causes are modifiable / Early beats late / Assessment can start from home).\n- Section \"Built around trust\": 4 cards (Private by design, Culturally respectful, Clinically bounded, Human oversight).\n- A \"Try a demo case\" row with three demo persona buttons (Low risk / Moderate risk / Urgent red flag) that instantly pre-fill the whole assessment and jump to results. This is for the judges\' demo video, so make it prominent but tidy.\n\nSCREEN 2: CONSENT (/consent) shown before the assessment\n- Title \"Your data, your rules.\"\n- Two-column plain-language panel: \"What HAYAT does with your answers\" vs \"What HAYAT never does\".\n  Does: processes answers to produce your screening, keeps results only on your device unless you choose otherwise, lets you delete everything anytime.\n  Never: sells data, shares with employers/insurers/family, stores your name, makes a diagnosis.\n- Granular consent toggles, all OFF by default except the first one which is required:\n  1. (Required) \"Process my answers to generate my screening.\"\n  2. \"Save my results on this device so I can come back.\"\n  3. \"Share an anonymised summary to help improve fairness research.\"\n  4. \"Allow me to create a summary I can share with a clinician (I decide when).\"\n- A \"Data minimisation\" note: \"We only ask what is needed. Every question can be skipped.\"\n- Button \"I understand, continue\" disabled until toggle 1 is on. Record consent with a timestamp in app state.\n\nSCREEN 3: ASSESSMENT WIZARD (/check) \342\200\224 6 steps with a progress bar, back/next, autosave, step transitions\nEVERY question has: a \"Why we ask\" expandable tooltip and a \"Prefer not to say\" option. Never block progression on skipped questions.\nStep 1 \"About you\": age range (18-24, 25-29, 30-34, 35-39, 40-44, 45+), language is already chosen, \"Are you planning or trying for a family?\" (Not now / Planning / Trying).\nStep 2 \"Your journey\": if Trying, how long (under 3 months, 3-6, 6-12, over 12 months); \"Has a partner been assessed?\" (Yes / No / Not applicable / Prefer not to say); \"Any earlier fertility tests?\" (None / Semen analysis / Hormone tests / Other).\nStep 3 \"Daily life\": smoking/vaping (Never / Past / Occasional / Daily), alcohol (None / Light / Moderate / Heavy), sleep hours slider, exercise days per week slider, stress level 1-5, heat exposure (frequent hot baths/saunas, long laptop on lap, hot work environment: multi-select), supplements/anabolic steroid or testosterone use (None / Past / Current), recreational substance use (None / Occasional / Regular).\nStep 4 \"Body & metabolic\": height and weight (auto-compute BMI, show it softly), known conditions (multi-select: diabetes or high blood sugar, high blood pressure, thyroid issue, high cholesterol, none), waist concern (optional).\nStep 5 \"History & symptoms\": past mumps, past genital or urinary infection, any genital surgery or injury, undescended testicle as a child (all Yes/No/Not sure/Prefer not to say). Current symptoms (multi-select): a feeling of heaviness or swollen veins, a lump, dull ache, reduced sex drive, difficulty with erections, none. RED-FLAG symptoms (multi-select, shown with a calm amber style): sudden severe testicular pain, rapid swelling, fever with scrotal pain, visible blood in semen, a hard painless lump.\nStep 6 \"Test results (optional)\": \"Do you already have a semen analysis?\" If yes, inputs for volume (mL), concentration (million/mL), total motility (%), progressive motility (%), plus optional hormones (FSH, total testosterone) with units. Show a soft note: \"Skip this if you do not have results. HAYAT works without them.\"\nFinal button \"See my screening\".\n\nSCREEN 4: ANALYSING (transition, about 2.5 seconds)\nElegant animated screen with rotating reassurance lines (\"Weighing your lifestyle factors\", \"Checking for red flags first\", \"Preparing explanations in plain language\"). Red-flag check message appears first to signal safety-first design.\n\nSCREEN 5: RESULTS (/results) \342\200\224 the hero screen, make it spectacular\nTop: a clear amber/blue banner \"This is a screening, not a diagnosis.\"\nSection A, Risk Overview: animated circular gauge with the risk band (Low / Moderate / Elevated / Urgent) coloured per palette, plus a 1-sentence plain-language summary. Show the score as a band first; the numeric score is secondary.\nSection B, \"Why this result?\" (EXPLAINABILITY PANEL): a horizontal contribution bar chart of the top factors, each with direction (raises / lowers / neutral), a short plain-language explanation, a tag \"You can change this\" or \"Not changeable\", and the value the user gave. Toggle between \"Simple\" and \"Detailed\" explanation views. Also list \"Factors working in your favour\". Include a \"What we did NOT use\" line (name, religion, location).\nSection C, Screening vs Diagnosis card: three short points on what this result can and cannot tell him.\nSection D, Red flags: if any red flag is selected, show a prominent, calm but firm urgent card at the very top of the page with \"Please seek medical assessment today\" and why. Never hide this.\nSection E, Recommended next step (TRIAGE): a 5-level ladder (Self-care \342\206\222 Primary care \342\206\222 Andrology/Urology \342\206\222 Fertility service \342\206\222 Urgent assessment) with the recommended level highlighted and a one-line reason. Beneath it, \"Clinical boundaries and human oversight\": states which cases always require a human clinician, and that HAYAT never replaces one.\nSection F, Action plan: \"Things you can start this week\" (3-5 modifiable-factor actions tailored to the answers) and \"Questions to ask a doctor\" list.\nSection G, Buttons: \"Open Care Navigator\", \"Create clinician summary (you decide who sees it)\" which opens a clean printable summary modal with a Print/Save-as-PDF button, only enabled if consent toggle 4 was on (otherwise prompt to enable it), and \"Delete my data now\".\n\nSCREEN 6: CARE NAVIGATOR (/care)\n- Step-by-step pathway cards matching the triage level, with \"What to expect\", \"Typical visit\", \"How to prepare\".\n- Low-friction options: \"Private teleconsultation\" (mock booking widget with date/time slots and a discreet-appointment-label option), \"At-home steps where clinically appropriate\" (clear note that clinician confirms suitability), \"Find a clinic\" (mock list of 4 clinics with distance, services, \"male-friendly / private entrance / female or male clinician preference\" filters).\n- Referral tracker: a simple status timeline (Screened \342\206\222 Booked \342\206\222 Assessed \342\206\222 Follow-up) with mark-as-done buttons, saved locally. This supports the \"referral completion\" metric.\n\nSCREEN 7: PRIVACY DASHBOARD (/privacy)\n- \"Everything HAYAT holds about you\" data inventory table (field, value, stored where, purpose) with per-item delete.\n- Consent toggles (same four) that can be changed any time, with an immediate effect.\n- Buttons: \"Export my data (JSON)\", \"Delete everything now\" (confirmation modal, then wipes all local state and shows a success state).\n- Access log: a simple list of events (\"Assessment created\", \"Summary generated\", \"Consent changed\") with timestamps.\n- A plain-language \"How your data is protected\" accordion (data minimisation, no identifiers, encryption planned in transit and at rest in the full version, access controls, retention limits).\n\nSCREEN 8: EVIDENCE (/evidence) \342\200\224 demonstrates value\nClearly labelled \"SIMULATED EVALUATION: 500 synthetic cases, not real patients\".\n- KPI cards (animated count-up): Assessment completion rate (e.g. 91%), Median time to appropriate evaluation (before 7.5 months vs with HAYAT 2.5 months), Referral completion rate (e.g. 68%), User trust score (e.g. 4.5/5), Red-flag recall (e.g. 100% of simulated red-flag cases routed to urgent).\n- Charts (Recharts): completion funnel by step, time-to-evaluation before vs after, triage distribution of the 500 cases, and a FAIRNESS section: grouped bar charts of completion rate, correct triage rate and trust score across age groups (18-29, 30-39, 40+) and across language groups (English, Hindi, Arabic), with a \"max gap\" indicator and a green tick if the gap is under 5 percentage points.\n- A \"Method\" accordion explaining the synthetic data generation and metrics honestly.\n- Generate this data from a seeded deterministic function in src/data/syntheticCohort.ts so it is reproducible, and expose it via the service layer.\n\nSCREEN 9: HOW IT WORKS / SAFETY (/how-it-works)\n- Animated 4-step explainer.\n- \"How the risk screening works\": transparent list of the rule categories and a plain statement that it is a rule-based, explainable model, not a black box.\n- \"Safety and limits\": what HAYAT can and cannot do, red-flag list, when to call a doctor immediately, and the human-oversight policy.\n- \"Fairness and cultural respect\" section.\n- FAQ accordion (8 questions, including \"Is this a diagnosis?\", \"Who can see my data?\", \"Why do you ask about lifestyle?\").\n\nSCREEN 10: QUICK-HIDE neutral screen (described in 4A).\n\n=====================================================\n6. RISK ENGINE (src/engine/riskEngine.ts) \342\200\224 transparent and explainable\n=====================================================\nWrite a pure, well-typed, well-commented function:\nassess(answers) => { band, score (0-100), factors[], redFlags[], triage, favourableFactors[], missingInfoNote }\n\nEach factor object: { id, label, direction: \'raises\'|\'lowers\'|\'neutral\', weight (number), modifiable (boolean), userValue (string), explanation (plain language, gentle) }.\n\nRED FLAGS (checked FIRST and override everything): sudden severe testicular pain, rapid swelling, fever with scrotal pain, visible blood in semen, hard painless lump. Any red flag sets band = \"Urgent\" and triage = \"Urgent assessment\". Pain with swelling is time-critical, so wording must say seek care today.\n\nRule-based weights (sum, then cap 0-100) for these factors, using simple general-knowledge reasoning:\n- Trying 12+ months (strong), 6-12 months (moderate)\n- Age 40+ (mild), 45+ (mild-moderate)\n- Daily smoking (moderate), heavy alcohol (moderate), moderate alcohol (mild)\n- BMI 30+ (moderate), BMI 25-29.9 (mild), BMI under 18.5 (mild)\n- Diabetes/high blood sugar (moderate), thyroid issue (mild)\n- Frequent heat exposure (mild)\n- Current/past anabolic steroid or testosterone use (strong when current)\n- Regular recreational substance use (mild-moderate)\n- Poor sleep under 6h (mild), high stress 4-5 (mild)\n- Past mumps (mild), past genital/urinary infection (moderate), surgery/injury (mild), undescended testicle history (moderate)\n- Symptoms of heaviness/swollen veins (moderate, may suggest a varicocele), dull ache (mild)\n- Semen analysis, using WHO 2021 lower reference limits: volume under 1.4 mL, concentration under 16 million/mL, total motility under 42%, progressive motility under 30% each add significant weight if entered. Words used must say \"below the reference range\", never \"infertile\".\n- Protective/lowering factors: regular exercise 3+ days, non-smoker, healthy BMI 18.5-24.9, good sleep, low stress.\nBands: 0-24 Low, 25-49 Moderate, 50-74 Elevated, 75+ Elevated-high still \"Elevated\" (Urgent is ONLY from red flags).\nTriage mapping: Low \342\206\222 Self-care; Moderate \342\206\222 Primary care; Elevated \342\206\222 Andrology/Urology; Elevated + trying 12+ months or abnormal semen values \342\206\222 Fertility service; any red flag \342\206\222 Urgent assessment.\nAlways include a missingInfoNote when optional sections were skipped (\"This screening is based on what you shared. More information can make it more accurate.\").\nNEVER output words like \"you have\", \"diagnosed with\", \"infertile\", \"impotent\". Use screening language only.\n\nAlso provide 3 demo personas (src/data/personas.ts): Low risk (29, healthy, trying 2 months), Moderate risk (36, BMI 28, smoker, trying 8 months), Urgent (31, sudden severe pain and swelling flagged).\n\n=====================================================\n7. SERVICE LAYER (so my backend can be plugged in later)\n=====================================================\nCreate src/services/api.ts exporting async functions with realistic delays and clear \"TODO: replace with real backend endpoint\" comments, each with typed request/response interfaces in src/types:\n- assess(answers): POST /api/assess\n- explain(result, mode, language): POST /api/explain (returns plain-language explanation text; mock uses templates)\n- saveConsent(consent): POST /api/consent\n- getConsentLog(): GET /api/consent-log\n- deleteAllData(): DELETE /api/data\n- exportData(): GET /api/export\n- getEvidence(): GET /api/evidence (returns the synthetic cohort metrics)\n- getClinics(filters): GET /api/clinics\n- bookTeleconsult(slot): POST /api/teleconsult\nRead the base URL from import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE with a fallback to mock mode, with a single flag VITE_USE_MOCK=true. State lives in a small store (Zustand or React Context) persisted to localStorage ONLY if consent toggle 2 is on; otherwise memory only.\n\n=====================================================\n8. CODE QUALITY AND STRUCTURE\n=====================================================\nFolder structure: src/{components, pages, engine, services, data, i18n, store, hooks, types, styles}. Reusable components (GlassCard, Gauge, StepWizard, ConsentToggle, FactorBar, TriageLadder, QuickHide, LangSwitcher, KpiCard, SafetyFooter). Strong TypeScript types, no any. Include a README.md describing the app, structure, how to run, and the mock-to-backend swap. Include a .env.example.\n\n=====================================================\n9. FINISH CHECKLIST (do ALL of this yourself before replying)\n=====================================================\n1. Run the app and make sure there are zero console errors and zero TypeScript errors.\n2. Test every screen and flow: landing \342\206\222 consent \342\206\222 all 6 wizard steps \342\206\222 analysing \342\206\222 results; all three demo personas; red-flag override; Quick Hide and Escape key; language switch including Arabic RTL; light/dark theme; privacy dashboard delete and export; clinician summary modal; evidence charts.\n3. Check mobile (375px) and desktop (1440px) layouts and fix any overflow or overlap.\n4. Fix every issue you find yourself.\n5. Then reply with ONE final message: a short summary of what was built, the file structure, and anything I should know. Do not ask me questions."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Do ALL of the following yourself in one pass, with no questions. When finished, run the app, verify every item below, fix anything that fails, and report once with everything done.\n\nA. LOGIC AND BUGS\n1. Make the wizard conditional: show \"How long have you been actively trying to conceive?\" ONLY if the user chose \"Actively trying currently\". If they chose \"Not right now\" or \"Planning for the future\", skip it and show a gentle line instead (e.g., \"Planning ahead is a great time to check in.\"). The risk engine must not count a timeline the user never entered, and the Privacy Dashboard must match the user\'s real answer.\n2. Stop showing raw enum values anywhere in the UI (under_3, 18-24, never, etc.). Add a single label-mapping helper and use human-readable text everywhere (Results, Privacy Dashboard data inventory, clinician summary, export preview).\n3. Fix the top navigation so it never wraps: use whitespace-nowrap, tighter spacing, move the \"FRONT DOOR\" tag out of the nav row (or hide below xl), and switch to a hamburger menu below 1280px. It must look clean at 375px, 768px, 1280px and 1440px, including when the Results link is visible.\n4. Make the results wording consistent with the factors found. The triage reasoning text must be generated from the actual factors (if risk factors exist, say \"a few modifiable factors were found; small changes may help\", never \"strong protective factors\" alone).\n\nB. MISSING FIELDS (add them)\n5. Step 3: add \"Stress level\" (1-5 selector) and \"Recreational substance use\" (None / Occasional / Regular, with Prefer not to say). Wire both into the risk engine (stress 4-5 mild raise, regular substance use mild-moderate raise) and into the explainability panel.\n6. Step 4: add an optional \"Waist size concern\" (Yes/No/Prefer not to say). Show BMI softly under height/weight once both are entered (\"Your BMI is about 24.5, in the typical range\" style, never judgemental), and make sure BMI feeds the engine.\n7. Make all wizard and page containers use one consistent max width. Wizard cards should be wider on desktop (about max-w-3xl) and centered.\n\nC. TRUST AND COPY\n8. Replace all real hospital/clinic names with clearly fictional ones (e.g., \"Al Noor Men\'s Health Clinic\", \"Marina Andrology Centre\"). Change \"Find a verified clinic\" to \"Sample clinics (demo data)\". Add a small \"Sample data\" badge on the list. Remove invented wait times or label them \"illustrative\".\n9. Soften all medical claims to cautious language (\"may\", \"is associated with\"). Remove claims like \"optimal support for Leydig cell testosterone production\" and \"peak spermatogenesis potential\".\n10. Rewrite clinical jargon into plain language a non-medical person understands. In the Privacy Dashboard, the \"Clinical Purpose\" column becomes \"Why we use it\" with simple wording. Replace the header \"Unilateral User Control\" with \"You\'re in control\".\n11. On the Evidence page, relabel Red-Flag Recall as \"Red-flag safety rule: 20/20 urgent cases routed to urgent assessment (guaranteed by design, rule-based override)\". Fix the funnel chart so every axis label is fully readable (larger font, wider label column, no overlap). Generate the evidence data by running the 500 synthetic cases through the REAL risk engine (seeded, deterministic) instead of hard-coded numbers, and keep the \"Simulated evaluation, not real patients\" label.\n\nD. READABILITY\n12. Increase the minimum body font size to 14px (captions 12px minimum), raise muted text and placeholder contrast to meet WCAG AA on both the dark and light themes, and increase the size of the footer disclaimer text.\n\nE. VERIFY (do these yourself and fix failures)\nTest: landing page; all 3 demo personas; the red-flag path (must show the urgent card at the very top and Urgent triage); Quick Hide button and Esc key; English, Hindi and Arabic (Arabic must flip to RTL with no layout breakage); light and dark themes; all wizard branches (Not now / Planning / Trying); Privacy Dashboard delete + export; clinician summary modal; Evidence page charts and fairness section; widths 375, 768, 1280 and 1440. Zero console errors, zero TypeScript errors."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "You are a senior product designer and creative front-end engineer. Redesign the VISUAL and INTERACTION layer of the existing HAYAT app. Do everything yourself in one pass, ask me nothing, run the app, fix every error, verify all items at the end, and report once with everything finished. Do NOT change the risk engine rules, the service layer, the i18n keys, or any safety/consent logic. Only upgrade the UI layer, and keep every existing feature working.\n\n1. VISUAL IDENTITY\n- Make a warm \"sand\" LIGHT theme the DEFAULT: background #E8E1D5, surface #F4EFE6, card #FBF8F2, primary text #2B2420, muted text #6B5F57 (must pass WCAG AA), primary brand taupe #7A645C, accent teal #1F8A7D for positive/interactive states. Keep a polished DARK theme as a toggle (deep warm charcoal, not neon).\n- Risk band colours stay calm and accessible (Low green, Moderate amber, Elevated orange, Urgent red) and are never the only signal (always add an icon + text).\n- Typography: headings \"Outfit\" with large confident sizes, optional serif accent \"Fraunces\" for hero emphasis words, body \"Inter\" at 16px minimum. For Hindi use \"Noto Sans Devanagari\", for Arabic use \"IBM Plex Sans Arabic\". Verify all three languages render cleanly, Arabic in RTL.\n- Replace the neon glass-card look with soft, matte, paper-like cards: subtle shadow, 20-24px radius, generous whitespace, hairline borders. No heavy glow.\n\n2. HERO WITH WEBGL (landing page)\n- Build a full-width hero with an animated dotted wave using react-three-fiber (@react-three/fiber, @react-three/drei): a Points mesh of about 6,000 points displaced by layered sine waves, colored taupe on sand, slowly flowing, with subtle mouse/touch parallax.\n- Lazy-load it with React.lazy + Suspense. Cap devicePixelRatio at 1.5, pause rendering when the tab is hidden, and use frameloop=\"demand\" or throttle where possible.\n- Provide a static CSS/SVG dotted-wave fallback for: prefers-reduced-motion, no WebGL support, or low-power devices. If the 3D packages cannot be installed in this environment, implement the same effect with a 2D canvas particle wave instead.\n- Headline over it: \"A private first step for men\'s reproductive health.\" Keep the sublines, the two buttons, the trust pills and the demo-persona row.\n\n3. WIZARD REDESIGN\n- ONE question per screen with a large heading, big tappable option cards (min 56px tall), a \"Why we ask\" expander and a visible \"Prefer not to say / Skip\" on every question.\n- Auto-advance after a single-select answer (short 250ms delay, with Back always available). Keyboard: number keys select options, Enter continues, Esc triggers Quick Hide.\n- Progress indicator shaped as a thin wave line that fills as the user advances, with \"Question X of N\" text.\n- Keep all existing questions, conditional logic and fixes. Group them under the same 6 section titles shown as small chapter labels.\n- Smooth slide/fade transitions with Framer Motion, respecting prefers-reduced-motion. Preserve autosave.\n\n4. RESULTS AS A SCROLL STORY\n- Use GSAP + ScrollTrigger (or Framer Motion scroll) to reveal sections in order: (1) the red-flag card if applicable (always first and prominent), (2) the \"screening, not diagnosis\" banner, (3) the risk band gauge animating from 0 to the value, (4) \"Why this result?\" factor bars animating in one by one with direction icons (raises/lowers) and \"You can change this\" tags, (5) the 5-level triage ladder as a vertical path with a \"You are here\" marker and the reason, (6) the action plan and \"questions to ask a doctor\" cards, (7) buttons.\n- Add an interactive WHAT-IF panel: sliders/toggles for modifiable factors (exercise days, sleep hours, smoking, alcohol, laptop/heat exposure, stress). Changing them re-runs the existing on-device risk engine live and shows the before -> after band and score with an animated transition. Label it clearly: \"Illustration only, not medical advice.\"\n- Keep \"Simple / Detailed\" explanation toggle and the \"What we did NOT use\" line.\n\n5. OTHER SCREENS\n- Care Navigator: card layout with a simple stepper timeline (Screened -> Booked -> Assessed -> Follow-up). Clearly label clinics as sample data.\n- Privacy Dashboard: friendlier table with plain-language columns, a visible \"What left your device\" section, and clear Export / Delete buttons.\n- Evidence: cleaner charts with direct labels, no squeezed axes, an honest \"Method and limits\" panel. State clearly that simulated results illustrate how the rules behave and do not prove real-world accuracy.\n- Quick Hide: keep the neutral wellness screen, make the button more prominent on mobile, and add a double-tap-on-logo shortcut in addition to Esc.\n\n6. PERFORMANCE AND ACCESSIBILITY\n- Route-level code splitting, lazy-loaded 3D chunk, no layout shift, images/SVGs optimised.\n- WCAG AA contrast in both themes, visible focus rings, full keyboard navigation, aria labels, 44px tap targets, prefers-reduced-motion respected everywhere.\n\n7. VERIFY YOURSELF AND FIX FAILURES\nLanding + WebGL hero + fallback; wizard all branches with auto-advance and keyboard; demo personas; red-flag path; what-if panel updates; Quick Hide (button, Esc, logo double-tap); English/Hindi/Arabic (RTL); light and dark; Privacy export/delete; Evidence; widths 375, 768, 1280, 1440; zero console and TypeScript errors."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "You are a senior creative developer and motion designer. Take the existing HAYAT app and make the WHOLE site feel cinematic, premium and alive, while staying calm, trustworthy and safe for a sensitive health context. Do everything yourself in one pass, ask me nothing, run the app, fix every error, verify everything at the end, and report once. Do NOT change risk engine rules, service layer, i18n keys or consent logic. Keep all existing features working.\n\n=== 0. MOTION PRINCIPLES (apply everywhere) ===\n- Calm and confident: durations 300-700ms, easing cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1). No bounce, no flashing, nothing above 3 flashes/second.\n- Respect prefers-reduced-motion everywhere (replace with simple fades, disable smooth scroll, parallax, particles, cursor effects).\n- SAFETY EXCEPTIONS: the red-flag urgent card appears INSTANTLY with no reveal delay or scroll-gating, and Quick Hide (button, Esc, logo double-tap) switches INSTANTLY with no transition.\n- Performance: 60fps target, only ONE WebGL context in the whole app, lazy-load heavy chunks, pause animations when off-screen or tab hidden, cap devicePixelRatio at 1.5. On touch/mobile devices use lighter variants (fewer particles, no custom cursor, no smooth-scroll library).\n\n=== 1. ANIMATION SYSTEM ===\n- Install and use: gsap (+ ScrollTrigger), lenis (smooth scroll, desktop only, disabled for reduced motion), framer-motion (route transitions, layout and presence animations), @react-three/fiber + @react-three/drei (hero only). If any package cannot be installed here, implement the effect with CSS/Canvas2D and keep going.\n- Create a reusable toolkit in src/motion/: <Reveal> (fade-up on enter), <SplitText> (line/word reveal for headings, accessible, no broken screen-reader text), <CountUp>, <Magnetic> (subtle magnetic buttons, desktop only), <TiltCard> (gentle 3D hover tilt, desktop only), <PathDraw> (SVG stroke draw on scroll), a usePrefersReducedMotion hook, and a shared easing/duration config.\n- Soft cursor glow follower on desktop only (a low-contrast warm halo, hidden on touch and reduced motion).\n- Route transitions: a calm full-page transition using a sweeping dotted-wave mask or a soft fade/slide (Framer Motion AnimatePresence), under 600ms, skipped for reduced motion. Scroll resets to top on route change.\n- A subtle site-wide background: a very low-contrast animated dotted wave (Canvas2D, cheap) that slowly shifts hue with the current page, and tints toward the risk band color on the Results page. Must never reduce text contrast.\n\n=== 2. PAGE-BY-PAGE ===\nLANDING: Keep the current hero and design I like; make it more alive. Hero WebGL dotted wave (react-three-fiber Points, about 6,000 points, mouse/touch parallax, lazy-loaded, with static SVG fallback). Headline revealed line by line. Below it, build a PINNED scroll-driven story (GSAP ScrollTrigger) in 4 beats: (1) \"Today: men often enter the fertility pathway late\", (2) a timeline that fills slowly showing a long delay, (3) \"With a private first step\", the timeline compresses, (4) the CTA \"Start my private check\". Label any time figures as \"illustrative / simulated\". Trust pills slide in with stagger. \"Why earlier matters\" and \"Built around trust\" cards use <Reveal> + <TiltCard>. Demo persona buttons get a hover lift and a short micro-animation on click.\n\nCONSENT: Animated explainer illustration (SVG): a device icon with a shield, and arrows showing answers staying on the device (draws on enter). Toggles use a smooth animated switch with a small confirmation tick. The \"I understand, continue\" button animates from disabled to enabled when the required toggle turns on.\n\nWIZARD: Keep one-question-per-screen. Add a directional slide/fade between questions (forward slides left, back slides right), selection animation (green border, soft fill, animated checkmark drawn in), a wave-shaped progress line that fills smoothly, and the keyboard hint bar. FIX: number-key and Enter shortcuts MUST be disabled while any text/number input, slider or textarea is focused (so typing height/weight never triggers answers).\n\nANALYSING: Replace the plain spinner with a signature sequence: scattered dots converge into a ring that becomes the result gauge, while the status lines cross-fade (\"Checking for red flags first\", \"Weighing your lifestyle and metabolic factors\", \"Preparing explanations\"). Total 2.5 seconds maximum, skippable with a \"Skip\" link, simple fade for reduced motion.\n\nRESULTS (scroll story): Order: (1) red-flag card if any (instant), (2) \"screening, not a diagnosis\" banner, (3) gauge morph from 0 to the value with the band label and a short plain summary, (4) \"Why this result?\" factor bars animating in one by one with direction icons and \"You can change this\" tags, (5) triage ladder as a vertical PathDraw line with a \"You are here\" marker that pulses softly, (6) action plan and \"questions for your doctor\" cards, (7) buttons. Make sure the interactive WHAT-IF panel exists: sliders/toggles for exercise days, sleep, smoking, alcohol, heat exposure (laptop/sauna), stress; changing them re-runs the existing on-device engine live and animates before -> after band and score, labelled \"Illustration only, not medical advice\". Tint the page accent subtly by band.\n\nCARE NAVIGATOR: Animated 4-step stepper (Screened -> Booked -> Assessed -> Follow-up) with a progress line that fills as steps are marked done. Clinic cards use <TiltCard> and <Reveal>. Label ALL clinics \"Sample data\" and remove or label distances as \"illustrative\". Make teleconsultation a clearly mock booking with NO personal fields (no name/phone/email).\n\nPRIVACY DASHBOARD: Add an animated data-flow diagram titled \"What left your device\" (shows \"Nothing\" with a check in the default state, and updates if any optional sharing consent is on). The Delete everything action plays a memorable but calm \"dissolve\" where the data rows break into particles and fade, then shows a success state. Export (JSON) must show a clear notice first: \"This file contains sensitive health information. Store it carefully.\" and include the same notice at the top of the JSON file in a _notice field.\n\nEVIDENCE: KPI cards count up when scrolled into view. Charts animate in on enter, with direct labels, readable axes and no overlapping text. Fairness section shows group bars with a \"max gap\" indicator and a tick when under 5 percentage points. Keep the labels \"Simulated evaluation, not real patients\" and the method note stating that simulated results illustrate how the rules behave and do not validate clinical accuracy. Remove any wording like \"validation\", \"proof\" or \"accuracy proven\" anywhere in the app.\n\nHOW IT WORKS: A pinned 4-step animated explainer (Private intake -> Rule-based engine -> Explainable guidance -> Targeted triage), each step highlighted as the user scrolls, with a small diagram per step. Add a compact \"Safety and limits\" section and an FAQ accordion with smooth height animation.\n\nQUICK HIDE: Instant switch (no animation). Keep the neutral wellness screen and the Return link.\n\n=== 3. POLISH ===\n- Consistent 8px spacing scale, consistent card radius, consistent button styles with hover/press/focus states.\n- Custom skeleton/shimmer loading states, friendly empty states, and a tidy 404 route.\n- Page <title>, meta description, favicon and an OG image for sharing. Production build must pass with zero warnings that break the build.\n- Hindi (Noto Sans Devanagari) and Arabic (IBM Plex Sans Arabic) must render cleanly; Arabic flips layouts to RTL including animation directions (slides and progress lines mirror).\n\n=== 4. VERIFY YOURSELF AND FIX FAILURES ===\nTest: landing pinned story + hero fallback; consent animation; every wizard branch incl. typing height/weight with shortcuts active; the analysing sequence and Skip; results for all 3 demo personas; the red-flag path (urgent card instant, Urgent triage); what-if panel; care stepper; privacy export notice + delete dissolve; evidence counters and charts; how-it-works pinned explainer; Quick Hide via button, Esc and logo double-tap; English/Hindi/Arabic (RTL); light and dark themes; prefers-reduced-motion on; mobile 375px, tablet 768px, desktop 1280 and 1440; zero console errors, zero TypeScript errors, successful production build. Then reply ONCE with a short summary of what was built and anything I should know."
    }
  }
}
